{"challenges":[{"attack":"Test the cancellation at the excluded value x=2, where x-2=0.","claim_id":"conditional-simplification","outcome":"survives","result":"The claim is explicitly quantified only over x≠2, so cancellation is applied only with a nonzero factor." ,"tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check whether the proposed domain omits any other real input or includes x=2.","claim_id":"original-domain","outcome":"survives","result":"x-2=0 exactly at x=2; thus precisely that input is excluded.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check the comparison at x=2 for an illicit equality claim.","claim_id":"domain-comparison","outcome":"survives","result":"R(2) is undefined because its denominator is zero, whereas x+2 evaluates to 4. Thus they do not have the same value at every real input.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Expand the asserted factorization, including signs.","claim_id":"numerator-factorization","outcome":"survives","result":"(x-2)(x+2)=x²+2x-2x-4=x²-4 for every real x.","tool_ids":[]}],"checks":[{"basis_reasoning":"The question explicitly supplies the expression and restricts consideration to real x where it is defined. The draft's definition merely names that quotient R.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":[],"claim_id":"given-expression","reasoning":"Supported as a restatement of the question premise.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The derivation is established by denominator-zero and establish-domain: x-2 is zero exactly at 2, and a real quotient requires a nonzero denominator.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["denominator-zero","establish-domain"],"claim_id":"original-domain","reasoning":"No numerator restriction applies to a quotient; the sole restriction is x≠2.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"factor-numerator directly verifies the identity by expansion.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["factor-numerator"],"claim_id":"numerator-factorization","reasoning":"Expansion yields x²-4 with no restriction on x.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"establish-domain supplies x-2≠0, factor-numerator supplies the substituted numerator, and cancel-on-domain then cancels a nonzero common factor.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["establish-domain","factor-numerator","cancel-on-domain"],"claim_id":"conditional-simplification","reasoning":"The universal quantifier is correctly restricted to real x≠2; there is no division-by-zero step.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"compare-excluded-input uses the established excluded input and directly evaluates x+2 at 2.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["establish-domain","cancel-on-domain","compare-excluded-input"],"claim_id":"domain-comparison","reasoning":"The two expressions agree on dom(R), but their domains differ: the polynomial is defined at 2 and the quotient is not. The wording 'not equal as functions defined at every real number' is imprecise because R is not defined on every real number, but its intended conclusion is correct.","verdict":"supported"}],"missing_evidence":[],"recommended_action":"finish","tool_requests":[]}
